/**
 * Spanish, the fourth language taught from a table rather than a column.
 *
 * Same layer and same reasoning as polishTranslations.ts: keyed on the German
 * text, so nothing in the packs is edited and no existing entry can break by
 * being touched. A card is only taught in Spanish if it is in here, which
 * makes this file the syllabus rather than a decoration.
 *
 * WHICH SPANISH. European Spanish, held to across every line. That is a
 * decision about grammar, not only vocabulary, and it cannot be half made:
 *   - vosotros for a familiar plural "you" — "¿Habéis comido?", not
 *     "¿Han comido ustedes?". ustedes stays for the formal plural.
 *   - the perfect for today's news, as Spain uses it: "Hoy he ido al médico",
 *     where Latin America would say "Hoy fui al médico".
 *   - Peninsular vocabulary where the two split: el coche (not el carro),
 *     el móvil (not el celular), el ordenador (not la computadora),
 *     el piso (not el departamento), coger (which is ordinary here).
 *
 * FORMS OF ADDRESS FOLLOW THE GERMAN. du → tú, ihr → vosotros, Sie → usted or
 * ustedes. A German sentence on Sie must not suddenly be on tú: the pair is
 * what the learner is being taught, and getting it wrong teaches them to be
 * rude in an office.
 *
 * THE STANDARD. Spoken Spanish, at the register the German uses. Set phrases
 * become set phrases, not word-for-word renderings:
 *   - Übung macht den Meister → La práctica hace al maestro.
 *   - Ich versteh nur Bahnhof → Me suena a chino. (the idiom, not the station)
 *   - Daumen drücken → cruzar los dedos.
 * Spanish drops the subject pronoun in speech — "Voy a casa", not "Yo voy a
 * casa" — except where the German is contrastive and the Spanish needs it too.
 *
 * German names and the names of German offices stay as they are wherever a
 * Spaniard living in Germany would also say them: "el Bürgeramt", "la
 * Anmeldung", "el Finanzamt". Translating those teaches a word nobody at the
 * counter will recognise.
 *
 * A literal translation no Spaniard would say is worse than none at all,
 * because the learner cannot tell the difference.
 */
